In this lesson we'll look at how to estimate a probability when there's no neat formula to work with. The trick is to run the experiment many times, count how often the event happens, and use that count as your estimate. We call this the relative frequency. As you repeat the trial more and more, that relative frequency starts to settle down and hover close to the true probability. So the recipe for a simulation goes like this. First, model the situation with something random, like rolling a die or tossing a coin. Then run the experiment heaps of times, count the successful outcomes, and divide by the total trials. That ratio is your probability estimate. Why bother with large samples? Because with only a handful of trials, the relative frequency jumps around a lot, but once you get up to a thousand trials or so, it usually settles to within a couple of percent of the real answer. Finally, you compare your simulated probability with the theoretical one if you know it. A small gap is just normal sampling variation, but a big gap means you've probably set the model up wrong. Now let's see it in action.